        <p><a href="/tool/image-compressor/" style="color:var(--accent);font-weight:700;">Open Free Image Compressor →</a></p>
      </div>

      <div class="tool-card">
        <h3>2. Squoosh — Best for Advanced Codec Control</h3>
        <p>Squoosh is a Google-built browser app that offers deep control over compression settings, including advanced codecs like AVIF and MozJPEG. It is excellent for developers and designers who want fine-grained control. The interface is more technical than ZippyWidgets.</p>
        <p><strong>Free features:</strong> JPG, PNG, WebP, AVIF compression, side-by-side preview, advanced settings.</p>
        <p><strong>Best for:</strong> Developers and designers who need precise control over output format and quality.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="tool-card">
        <h3>3. TinyPNG — Best for Simple Batch Compression</h3>
        <p>TinyPNG is famous for its smart lossy compression. It works well for photographs and supports batch uploads. The free version requires uploading files to TinyPNG's servers, which may not be ideal for sensitive images.</p>
        <p><strong>Free features:</strong> JPG, PNG, WebP compression, batch upload, simple interface.</p>
        <p><strong>Best for:</strong> Users who want a quick, well-known drag-and-drop compressor.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="tool-card">
        <h3>4. CompressJPEG — Best for Quick One-Off Compressions</h3>
        <p>CompressJPEG is a straightforward online compressor. It supports JPG, PNG and GIF files. It is free and easy to use, but it adds ads and requires uploading your images.</p>
        <p><strong>Free features:</strong> JPG, PNG, GIF compression, simple controls.</p>
        <p><strong>Best for:</strong> Users who need a fast, no-frills compression tool.</p>
