      <h2>JPEG vs PNG vs WebP — Which Should You Use?</h2>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>JPEG:</strong> Best for photographs and complex images. Offers the smallest file sizes with lossy compression. Not ideal for transparency.</li>
        <li><strong>PNG:</strong> Best for screenshots, graphics, logos, and anything needing transparency. Compression is lossless, so files are larger.</li>
        <li><strong>WebP:</strong> The modern choice. Smaller than JPEG and PNG in most cases while maintaining good quality. Supported by all major browsers.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How to Compress Images Without Losing Quality</h2>

      <div class="step-box">
        <strong>Tip 1:</strong> Start with the highest quality source image you have.
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <strong>Tip 2:</strong> Choose the right format for the content (JPEG for photos, PNG for graphics, WebP when supported).
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <strong>Tip 3:</strong> Set JPEG/WebP quality to 80-90% for a strong balance of size and quality.
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <strong>Tip 4:</strong> Resize images to the largest dimensions they will actually be displayed at.
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <strong>Tip 5:</strong> Preview the compressed image before downloading to make sure details are preserved.
      </div>

      <h2>Privacy: Why Client-Side Compression Matters</h2>

      <p>When you upload an image to a server, you lose control over it. The service may keep logs, thumbnails, or even the original file. Client-side tools like ZippyWidgets process images locally using the HTML5 canvas API. Your images never leave your device, making them safe for personal photos, client work, and confidential screenshots.</p>

      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <h3>What is the best free image compressor online?</h3>
      <p>For a free, private, no-limit experience, <a href="/tool/image-compressor/">ZippyWidgets Image Compressor</a> is the best choice. It runs entirely in your browser using the HTML5 canvas API, so your images are never uploaded. Squoosh is also excellent if you need advanced codec options.</p>

      <h3>Can I compress images without losing quality?</h3>
      <p>PNG compression is lossless, so quality is preserved. JPEG and WebP are lossy, meaning some quality is sacrificed for smaller file size. Use a quality setting of 80-90% for photos to keep quality high while significantly reducing file size.</p>

      <h3>Is it safe to upload images to online compressors?</h3>
      <p>When you upload an image to a server, you lose control over it. The service may keep logs, thumbnails, or even the original file. Client-side tools like ZippyWidgets process images locally using the HTML5 canvas API, so your images never leave your device.</p>

